# where zig fits in the c & rust discussion
# facts & versions checked Oct 2026 against zig 0.17.0 (released 2026-10-02).
# this file previously carried no date stamp and had drifted three releases;
# stamp any future edits so that stays visible.
# Oct 2026: bun left zig for rust — see the case study, it changes the
# conclusions here more than any version bump did.


# the one-line positioning

# - c:    trust the programmer; minimal abstractions; manual everything.
# - rust: never trust the programmer; the compiler proves memory safety; rich
#         type system.
# - zig:  trust the programmer, but give them better tools; no hidden control
#         flow, no hidden allocations, no hidden i/o — explicit everything.
#
# zig is essentially "c done right in 2026" — not "rust done lighter." that's
# the key distinction.


# what zig actually does differently

# 1. no hidden control flow. no operator overloading, no exceptions, no
#    destructors, no unwinding. if a function call is on the page, that's the
#    only thing happening. (rust has Drop and Deref; zig has neither.)
#    TWO OVERCLAIMS TO AVOID — this file made both:
#    - zig DOES have panics. @panic is a builtin and every runtime safety
#      check panics (see point 5). the defensible claim is "no EXCEPTIONS",
#      not "no panics".
#    - zig DOES have implicit coercions: integer widening, *[N]T -> []T,
#      T -> ?T, T -> !T, comptime_int resolution. what it lacks is implicit
#      NARROWING / lossy conversion.
#    THE "NO DESTRUCTORS" PART IS A REAL TRADEOFF, NOT A FREE WIN. `defer`
#    puts the burden at every call site; rust's Drop is automatic. that is
#    precisely the cost bun cited when it left zig — see the case study.
# 2. allocators are explicit. every function that allocates takes an allocator
#    as a parameter. there's no global malloc you accidentally call. you can
#    swap in a test allocator that detects leaks, an arena allocator for
#    short-lived work, a fixed-buffer allocator with no heap at all.
# 3. `comptime` instead of macros / generics. compile-time code is just zig
#    code that runs at compile time. generics are functions that take types as
#    values. no separate macro language, no separate trait system.
# 4. first-class c interop. @cImport literally parses c headers and gives you
#    the types directly — no ffi declarations, no bindgen step. zig can compile
#    c and c++ files; `zig cc` is a drop-in cross-compiler that many projects
#    use as their c toolchain (uber's go builds, for one — bun used to be the
#    headline example here, see the case study).
# 5. no memory safety guarantees at compile time. zig has runtime safety checks
#    in debug builds (bounds, overflow, ub) but no borrow checker.
#    use-after-free is still possible.
# 6. i/o is a parameter too, as of 0.16 (Apr 2026). std.Io is passed in
#    exactly the way an allocator is, so blocking vs async vs io_uring is a
#    value you choose at the call site rather than a property baked into the
#    function. THE REAL PRINCIPLE BEHIND POINTS 2 AND 6 IS THE SAME:
#    ambient capabilities become explicit parameters.
#    THIS IS CONTESTED, and on exactly the ground point 1 claims as zig's
#    win. critics argue an Io parameter IS function coloring by another
#    name — a function taking Io can't be called from one that doesn't have
#    an Io to hand, which is viral through a different mechanism. defenders
#    counter that Io is a concrete runtime value you can store in app state
#    and swap, unlike an `async` keyword. worth knowing the argument exists
#    before repeating "no hidden control flow" as settled.


# where zig is winning

# - NOT bun ANY MORE. it was the flagship zig application through 1.3.x and
#   shipped as rust in 1.4 (Aug 2026). this file used to lead with it. see
#   the case study below — it is the most informative data point here.
# - ghostty (mitchell hashimoto's terminal emulator) — zig.
# - tigerbeetle (financial database) — zig, leaning on explicit-allocator for
#   predictable performance.
# - river (wayland compositor), lots of game-dev and embedded experiments.
# - NOT uxn — this list used to claim it. the reference implementation is
#   ~150 lines of ANSI C, deliberately, so it runs on a GBA or a Pico. zig
